{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) to justify preferring better-corroborated theories without induction, but this fails because Popper's own formal definition of verisimilitude was proven logically incoherent by Tichý and Miller (1974). They demonstrated that under Popper's criteria, a false theory with more content cannot be ranked as closer to the truth than another false theory unless it entails the truth-content of the other, which is unknowable. Consequently, the 'pragmatic rule' the target proposes lacks the logical foundation it claims to have within Popper's system; without a coherent metric for verisimilitude, the preference for the 'better-corroborated' theory collapses back into an unjustified inductive assumption that past survival predicts future success or proximity to truth, exactly the smuggling of induction the prompt asks to identify.","premise":"Tichý and Miller (1974) proved that Popper's quantitative and qualitative definitions of verisimilitude are formally defective because they imply that any false theory is further from the truth than any other false theory that entails less content, or that comparisons between false theories are impossible without knowing the truth.","successor_question":"Given the collapse of the verisimilitude program, can Popperians defend the preference for corroborated theories solely on the basis of critical rationality (i.e., it is the only theory not yet refuted) without implicitly relying on the principle of uniformity of nature?","target":"08f954eb859da1ab5ea3cc6b3fe2ba94b9d6829f7379120a72eb99ef208b27ef"}]}